{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>To Abraham and to Isaac.</b> See what we explained in the portion of Toldot at the verse (Genesis 26:3) ״כִּי לְךָ וּלְזַרְעֲךָ״ etc., that Isaac was granted one additional merit beyond Abraham's portion, and that HaShem gave that too to Jacob." "<b>I will give et the land.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to repeat and say ״אֶתֵּן אֶת הָאָרֶץ״, and why it was not sufficient with what preceded: ״לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה וּלְזַרְעֶךָ״? Perhaps it intends to say that to him — to Jacob — He gave the land to hold possession of, whereas if He had said only ״לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה וּלְזַרְעֶךָ״ the intention would be that He gives it to him to bequeath it to his seed. Therefore Scripture was precise to state two givings: one for him and one for his seed — which it did not bother to state in the promise to the two fathers."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:10.423439096Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}